A||N||10||FieroSTETZ||problems maintaining oil pressure - help!||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||02-02-2001||02:55 AM||||i recently finished putting my 87gt all back together, and whiile it was all apart inatalled a new oil pressure sensor. today was my second day back on the road with it and something curious has been happening- my oil pressure is fine on startup, but when it heats up, the oil pressure is way down, almost in red, when idling too long it even puts on the oil pressure light- so, 60 miles from home i put in 2 qts of oil, no change in pressure, still too low. so, i finally get home, and let the car sit for a few hours, upon start, the car idles with the oil pressure over half, but after warming, it drops again. someone please tell me what to look for to help diagnose this!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Vlad||02-02-2001||03:10 AM||||<BR>BOA sending unit.<P>As an option.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||02-02-2001||07:39 AM||||Was the engine low on oil when you added more to it?What weight oil was in it originally,why did you change the sensor?If you overfill an engine with oil,the rods may hit the oil and whip it into a froth lowering the pressure,the viscosity of the oil can lower the pressure if the engine has a lot of wear,the engine idle speed will affect the oil pressure.Chances are the new sensor is okay,but new parts have been known to be bad.The gauge could also have a problem.If you can,check your connections. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||02-02-2001||12:42 PM||||How many miles on the engine?  Might just be time for a rebuild.. hook up a mechanical oil pressure gauge and check.<P>Could also just be time for an oil change.  Before I changed the oil in my GT (3,400 miles since last change) oil pressure was at about 40 at 2,000rpm and right next to red at idle.  Would get even lower after a long highway trip.  Changed the oil and went to 10w-40 and the pressure stays around 30-40 idle and 60-70 at 2,000.  154,000 on the engine.  My oil gauge is kinda flaky, though, and I'd like to check it with a mechanical gauge but I hafta buy one first...<P>It should always have a higher oil pressure when cold, since the oil is thicker.  If it's been too long since your last oil change the oil can thin out alot.||||reg||
Z||000004||FieroSTETZ||02-02-2001||02:07 PM||||could my oil pump be going? just wondeirn, 143k on the engine, just had the cradle out changing the clutch, manifolds, and all the sensors||||reg||1
Z||000005||LarryB||02-02-2001||03:56 PM||||Like Standard suggested, hook up a mechanical gauge and find out what's really going on.  At this point it's impossible to say that the pressure sensor/gauge is faulty or that you have a real problem starting. <BR>||||reg||3
Z||000006||FieroSTETZ||02-02-2001||09:02 PM||||well, i just checked my computer for codes and had a code 23 - MAT sensor (Manifold Absolute Temperature) (i think?) and a 32 - EGR system (Exhaust Gas Recirculation)- i also changed my oil to quaker state 10w30 synthetic blend and a fram heavy duty filter.. after restarting the car, i let it idle for about 20 minutes, allowing the temp to get to half, and the oil pressure was a little above half. i took it out for a drive, and within 10 minutes, i was sitting at a light and it was sub 20 psi @ 900 rpm (where my car idles) and was riding the line on the red zone. checked my oil when i got home, and was just right. could my oil pump be causing this, is this normal behaviour? (the cars oil pressure sensor has been bad since i bought it)||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroSTETZ||02-03-2001||01:32 PM||||i noticed that the car has a tapping sound in the engine that starts around 2700 rpm- could that be related? also, would it help if i put in a high volume oil pump?||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroSTETZ||02-04-2001||12:00 AM||||okay, put on a mechanical gauge, and it read pretty much the same as the dash one... so, what should i do now? i have this nagging feeling that the tapping sound at 2700 rpm is somehow related- hydraulic lifter stuck amybe? could this affect the oil pressure, or be caused by low pressure? im gonna try running kerosene through the engine and see if maybe the oil passages are clogged. some recommendations please!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1012904&a=7460757&p=25516673&Sequence=0&res=high"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Standard||02-04-2001||12:30 AM||||That tapping could be rod knock..  mine sounded pretty loose before I changed the oil, but that was only at idle.<P>Since you put in a new pressure sensor, I'm guessing the old one was bad, so you don't know how long the oil pressure has been low.  Hate to say it, but it sounds like it's time for a rebuild.  Bearings are getting too much clearance.  Putting in a high volume pump might help for a little bit, but it could also cause some gaskets to start puking.  I'd switch to some higher viscosity oil and take it easy on the engine until you can rebuild it.  Both of those are only band-aids.. it's all downhill from here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||
Z||000010||rockcrawl||02-04-2001||08:53 AM||||Common 2.8 problem. you're lucky you made it to 140k! Next thing to happen will be a spun rod bearing. Dump in some straight 50 wt oil and hope for the best, but prepare for a rebuild/replacement. A HV oil pump will not fix the problem. I had an 87 GT do the same thing at ~80k and another one spin a rod bearing at ~98k.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
